<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhaka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhaka is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, on the Buriganga river in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> delta. Made the Mughal capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Khan in 1610, it flourished on the muslin trade, and later became the capital of the province of Eastern Bengal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> from 1905 to 1911, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, and from 1971 of independent Bangladesh. It is one of the most densely populated cities in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhaka.jpg" alt="Dhaka" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meer Sadi · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Hatirjheel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dhaka lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> on the eastern bank of the Buriganga River, on the lower reaches of the Ganges Delta. The land is flat and close to sea level, and the city is bounded by the Buriganga, Turag, Dhaleshwari and Shitalakshya rivers, which leaves it susceptible to flooding in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> seasons. The origin of the name is uncertain. It may come from the dhak tree, once common in the area, from the goddess Dhakeshwari, or from the dhak, a drum played at the inauguration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> capital in 1610.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Urban settlements in the area date to the first millennium. The region belonged to the ancient district of Bikrampur, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-dynasty">Sena dynasty</a>, and under Islamic rule to the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a>. Isa Khan and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-khan-of-bengal">Musa Khan</a>, who ruled from Sonargaon, headed a confederation of twelve chieftains that resisted Mughal expansion in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in the late 16th century. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> changed course, the earlier capital Gour lost its strategic importance, and Dhaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> became the eastern frontiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><p>An early modern city developed from the 17th century as a provincial capital and commercial centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Dhaka was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Mughal Bengal</a> for 75 years, in two separate periods. It was the hub of the muslin trade and one of the most prosperous cities in the world, home to merchants from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. The Mughals named it Jahangirnagar in honour of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> and decorated it with gardens, tombs, mosques, palaces and forts. It was once called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of the East.</p><p>Under British rule Dhaka gained electricity, railways, Western-style universities and a modern water supply, and became an administrative and educational centre as capital of the province of Eastern Bengal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> after 1905. In 1947 it became the administrative capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> emptied the city of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population, which had come to form a majority during the previous century and dominated its economy. After the Liberation War of 1971 it became the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dhaka is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and the seat of its government. It has a heritage of about 2,000 buildings from the Mughal and British periods, and its most prominent landmark is the modernist Jatiya Sangsad Bhaban, designed by Louis Kahn. UNESCO has recognised the Bengali New Year parade, the Jamdani sari and rickshaw art as intangible cultural heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fine cotton cloth, including the muslins of the <b>Dhaka</b> region, was traded across the Indian Ocean, and the ports of Satgaon and Chittagong linked Bengal with Southeast Asia, China, the Persian Gulf and the Red Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1971 India intervened in the Bangladesh Liberation War, and within two weeks some 93,000 Pakistani soldiers surrendered at <b>Dhaka</b>, creating Bangladesh and confirming India's military primacy in South Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The All-India Muslim League, founded at <b>Dhaka</b> in 1906 to protect the interests of Muslims as a minority, won separate electorates in 1909 and agreed a joint programme with the Congress in the Lucknow Pact of 1916.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 March 2014, Bangladesh's independence day, 254,537 people sang it together at the National Parade Ground in <b>Dhaka</b>, a world record for the most people singing a national anthem at once, approved by Guinness World Records on 9 April 2014; India broke the record on 21 January…</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Pakistani army's crackdown in <b>Dhaka</b>, independence was proclaimed in the name of Sheikh Mujibur Rahman.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mughal governor Islam Khan moved the capital to <b>Dhaka</b> and defeated Musa Khan by 1611, absorbing eastern Bengal into the Mughal Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/baro-bhuyans">Baro-Bhuyans</a></li></ul></section>
